rows={1:[('Compare','the oscillation direction in transverse and longitudinal waves','Transverse oscillations are perpendicular to the direction of energy transfer; longitudinal oscillations are parallel.'),('Describe','particle motion in a sound wave travelling right through air','Air particles oscillate left and right about equilibrium, parallel to the energy transfer.'),('Describe','particle motion in a transverse rope wave travelling right','Rope particles oscillate up and down, perpendicular to energy transfer.'),('Compare','energy and matter transfer in both wave types','Both transfer energy; medium particles oscillate about equilibrium without net transport.'),('Explain','why a sound wave in air cannot be polarised','Its oscillations are parallel to propagation, so there is no transverse direction to select.'),('Compare','a transverse wave on a string and sound emitted by the string','The string oscillates transverse to wave travel along it; air particles oscillate longitudinally along sound travel.')],2:[('Identify','wavelength on a longitudinal particle-spacing diagram','It is the distance between centres of adjacent compressions or adjacent rarefactions.'),('Describe','what the vertical axis of a longitudinal displacement-distance graph represents','It is the signed displacement of particles parallel to the direction of wave travel.'),('Explain','the instantaneous motion of a particle at a crest of a transverse sinusoidal wave','It is momentarily stationary at maximum displacement before reversing direction.'),('Determine','particle motion at a positive spatial slope on a right-moving displacement-distance graph','The particle moves towards negative displacement because the right-moving profile shifts right.'),('Identify','a compression on a longitudinal displacement-distance graph','A compression lies where neighbouring particle displacements converge, at a negative spatial slope.'),('Explain','why a displacement-time graph cannot by itself identify a wave as transverse','Its vertical axis plots signed displacement in either wave type; the physical direction of oscillation must be specified.')]} 
